# {{full_name}}

An [Animus](https://github.com/launchapp-dev/animus-cli) trigger backend for **{{name}}**.

> **Status:** Scaffolded from
> [`animus-plugin-template`](https://github.com/launchapp-dev/animus-plugin-template).
> The example backend watches a directory and emits one event per
> debounced file change. Swap the `notify` watcher in `src/backend.rs`
> for any other event source (Slack socket, webhook listener, cron
> interval, message queue) — the trait surface does not change.

## What this is

A standalone stdio plugin that emits [`TriggerEvent`s](https://github.com/launchapp-dev/animus-protocol/blob/main/animus-trigger-protocol/src/lib.rs)
into the Animus daemon. Triggers are push-driven: the runtime calls
`trigger/watch` once, the plugin streams events as `trigger/event`
notifications, and workflows match on `kind` to dispatch work.

## Install

```bash
animus plugin install {{org}}/{{full_name}}
```

This downloads the latest release binary, verifies the SHA256, and
registers `{{full_name}}` with the daemon. Re-run after a release to
upgrade.

## Configure

Set the watch target:

```bash
export {{NAME_UPPER}}_WATCH_PATH="/path/to/watch"
# Optional:
export {{NAME_UPPER}}_DEBOUNCE_MS=250
export {{NAME_UPPER}}_RECURSIVE=true
```

Wire the plugin into a workflow:

```yaml
# .animus/workflows/standard.yaml
triggers:
  {{name}}-watch:
    plugin: {{full_name}}
    config:
      watch_path: ./inbox
      debounce_ms: 250
      recursive: true

workflows:
  - id: process-inbox
    trigger: {{name}}-watch
    phases: [triage, implementation]
```

When a file inside `./inbox` changes, the trigger emits a
`{{name}}_file_changed` event and the daemon enqueues the workflow.

## Run

```bash
animus daemon start
# Drop a file into the watched directory; the daemon enqueues the
# wired workflow within `debounce_ms`.
```

## Roadmap

The scaffold ships a complete, working example. To turn it into your
production trigger:

- [ ] Replace the `notify::recommended_watcher` in `src/backend.rs`
      with your real event source (HTTP listener, Slack socket,
      cron interval, ...).
- [ ] Update `TriggerSchema::kinds` and `event_kind()` to match the
      event taxonomy your source produces.
- [ ] If your source supports delivery confirmation, implement
      `TriggerBackend::ack` and set `supports_ack = true`.
- [ ] If your source survives restarts and exposes a cursor, set
      `supports_resume = true` and persist the cursor between runs.
- [ ] Add a contract test under `tests/` for every event kind you
      emit.

